# Spanish Dialect Classifier

This project implements a TF-IDF + Logistic Regression model to classify Spanish sentences as either Mexican Spanish (MX) or Peninsular Spanish (ES). The classifier is trained on sentence-level text data, including curated slang representative of each dialect. After training, the program provides an interactive mode where users can input sentences and receive real-time predictions.

## Project Structure

```text
finalproject/
├── data/
│   └── clean/
│       ├── mx/            # Mexican Spanish sentences (one per .txt file)
│       └── es/            # Spain Spanish sentences (one per .txt file)
├── dialect_classification.py   # Main classifier script
├── split_slang_both.py         # Script to split slang datasets into individual files
├── test_sentences.py           # Contains 10 (MEX & ES) obvious sentences, 5 (MEX & ES) ambiguous sentences
├── README.md 
```
## Requirements

Python 3.9+
scikit-learn
numpy
pandas

Install dependencies with:
pip install scikit-learn numpy pandas


## Data Preparation

All text data is preprocessed to:
- lowercase text
- remove punctuation and non-alphabetic characters
- filter sentences with fewer than four words
- balance the number of sentences per dialect

Each sentence is saved as an individual .txt file in its corresponding dialect directory.

## Quick Test Suite (test_sentences.py)

Before entering interactive mode, the script automatically evaluates the trained model on a small, hand-curated test suite:

10 MX “obvious” sentences

10 ES “obvious” sentences

5 neutral sentences expected to lean MX

5 neutral sentences expected to lean ES

The output prints predicted labels, probability scores, and accuracy per group. This is meant for qualitative debugging and checking whether the model is relying on dialectal cues rather than topic or place names.

## Interactive Usage

After the model finishes training, an optional interactive mode allows users to input custom Spanish sentences and inspect the model’s predicted dialect probabilities. This feature is intended for qualitative analysis and debugging, not for computing evaluation metrics.

Users must enter a sentence containing at least four words. Shorter inputs are rejected to ensure sufficient linguistic context.

```
Enter a Spanish sentence: No manches wey, ese celular está bien caro

Predicted dialect: MX
   MX probability: 0.549
   MX probability: 0.549
   ES probability: 0.451
```

This interactive testing helps reveal:
- how the model responds to informal or slang-heavy input,
- confidence differences across dialects,
- common error patterns and decision boundary behavior.

Exit using:

```
quit
```
## How to Run

From the project root directory:
```bash
python dialect_classification.py
```
From there it will print out the quick tests as well as the interactive usage will be available.

## Data Sources

This project uses a multi-source dataset designed to capture dialectal variation between Mexican Spanish (MX) and Peninsular Spanish (ES) while mitigating genre and register bias.

The dataset consists of the following components:

Spanish Wikipedia Dump
Full-text articles from the Spanish Wikipedia were used as a large-scale, publicly available source of written Spanish. Articles include indirect regional cues such as country-specific terminology and topic associations. Sentences were then filtered, cleaned, and balanced across dialects.

Project Gutenberg (Peninsular Spanish)
Public-domain Spanish texts from Project Gutenberg were used to supplement formal Peninsular Spanish. These texts provide grammatically consistent, edited sentences and serve as a formal baseline for ES.

Manually Curated Slang and Conversational Sentences
To capture informal and dialect-specific usage, a small set of manually written sentences was added for each dialect. These sentences incorporate regionally distinctive slang and conversational structures (e.g., “güey”, “órale” for Mexico; “tío”, “vale” for Peninsular Spanish) and were created using pedagogical resources curated by native speakers.

All data was preprocessed to remove punctuation and non-alphabetic characters, lowercase text, and ensure balanced representation across dialects. Each sentence is stored as an individual .txt file to support clean document-level classification.

## Evaluation

Model performance is evaluated using:
- Accuracy
- Precision
- Recall
- F1-score (per dialect)

Qualitative evaluation is performed by testing the model on unseen dialect-specific sentences and examining confidence scores and misclassification patterns.


